%N A171759 Decimal expansion of limit sqrt(2*sqrt(3*sqrt(5*sqrt(7*sqrt(11*...sqrt(prime(n))...))))).
%C A171759 The square of this constant is the nested radical of the primorials: sqrt(2*sqrt(6*sqrt(30*sqrt(210*...)))) = Product_{n>=1} A002110(n)^(1/2^n) = 9.1260822033320214517784..., because prime(j) divides A002110(n) exactly for n >= j and Sum_{n>=j} 1/2^n = 2/2^j. - _Pierre Colmant_, Nov 01 2026
%H A171759 G. C. Greubel, <a href="/A171759/b171759.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A171759 Equals Product_{n>=1} prime(n)^(1/2^n).
%F A171759 With c = this constant, log(c) = Sum_{n>=1} log(prime(n))/2^n = 1.1055682450309199111115944..., and 0 < log(c) - Sum_{k=1..n} log(prime(k))/2^k <= log(2*prime(n+1))/2^n by Bertrand's postulate. - _Pierre Colmant_, Nov 01 2026
%e A171759 3.0209406156579810285918984039129839338566639519054579903275222722773436019339...
%t A171759 f:= Product[Prime[n]^(1/2^n), {n, 1, 700}]; RealDigits[f, 10, 50][[1]] (* _G. C. Greubel_, Apr 04 2018 *)
